  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does an induction hob work?",
      "a": "A coil under the glass makes a rapidly changing magnetic field. It induces swirling currents in the base of a magnetic pan, and those currents heat the pan directly.",
      "asks": [
        "What is induction cooking?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an induction hob stay cool?",
      "a": "The glass itself is not heated by the coil. It only gets warm from the hot pan sitting on it, and cools quickly when the pan is removed.",
      "asks": [
        "Can you touch an induction hob?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do some pans not work on induction?",
      "a": "The pan base must be magnetic, like iron or most steels. Aluminium, copper and glass pans do not heat well unless they have a magnetic plate in the base. If a fridge magnet sticks, it will work.",
      "asks": [
        "How do I know if a pan is induction compatible?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is induction so fast?",
      "a": "The heat is made directly in the pan instead of in a flame or hot plate that must first warm up and then pass heat across, so less is lost on the way.",
      "asks": [
        "Is induction faster than gas?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an induction hob buzz?",
      "a": "The fast-changing magnetic field makes the pan and its layers vibrate slightly, which can cause a hum or buzz, especially on high power.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is my induction pan humming?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Induction hobs transfer roughly 85 percent of their energy into the pan, compared with about 40 percent for a gas flame.",
    "Induction only heats pans with a magnetic base, such as cast iron or magnetic stainless steel."
  ],
